 All notable protocol changes are recorded here. Versions follow Semantic
 Versioning for the complete specification set.
 
+## 1.0.0-rc.4 - 2026-07-21
+
+### Added
+
+- Canonical and legacy-filename manifest schema 6 with context-excluded
+  `build_roots` and the closed declarative build command
+  `{ "type": "build", "driver": "go-v1", "source_dir": "..." }`.
+- Build receipt schema 1 and install marker schema 2 for exact build input,
+  logical cache key, receipt, artifact, and currentness metadata.
+- Conformance claim schema 2 for protocol rc.4. Claim schema 1 and its cases
+  remain byte-frozen as historical rc.3 evidence.
+- The generated rc.4 Go fixture, expected build artifacts, schema cases,
+  `build-drivers.json`, and compiled-build additions to
+  `manager-lifecycle.json`.
+- Decision 0004, which standardizes only compile-only `go-v1`, rejects generic
+  package build hooks, and requires a new closed identifier and independent
+  threat review for every future driver.
+
+### Changed
+
+- Added a manager-owned, native, vendor-only, networkless Go pipeline. The
+  manager fixes the trusted toolchain, process graph, environment, native
+  target, flags, internal-link policy, staging output, and resource bounds;
+  package source may select only its declared build root and source directory.
+- Excluded every declared build root from agent-facing context and the
+  commit-keyed runtime copy before locale rendering, cache lookup, compiler
+  discovery, or dry-run reporting. Generated executables remain outside prompt
+  context and are exposed only through manager-created shims.
+- Required source validation, closure and collision planning, audit,
+  attestation, and revocation gates before cache lookup or compilation. Cache
+  misses build in operation-private staging before shared installation state
+  changes.
+- Made build-enabled installation, upgrade, and repair one serialized,
+  journaled publication transaction: cache publication and target mutation are
+  ordered, the consumer ledger commits last, and rollback runs under the same
+  manager-home lock in reverse commit order.
+- Defined compiler-free dry-run behavior. Dry-run may perform only
+  package-independent toolchain probes; it never runs `go list`, `go build`, a
+  compiler, or a linker and creates no persistent cache, lock, journal,
+  runtime, context, marker, shim, adapter, consumer, audit, registry, repair,
+  quarantine, or GC state.
+
+### Compatibility
+
+- Existing schema 1 through 5 packages require no migration and retain their
+  deployed behavior. Current readers continue to accept them; current writers
+  use schema 6 for new or rewritten manifests.
+- Schema 6, marker v2, receipt v1, and claim v2 are explicit version
+  transitions. Older readers must reject unsupported versions rather than
+  infer new behavior, partially install a build package, or fall back.
+- Managers read marker v1 and v2, write v2 after installation mutation, and
+  may keep a valid marker v1 current for an unchanged schema 1 through 5
+  installation.
+- Claim v1 remains rc.3-only evidence. rc.4 writers emit claim v2 tied to the
+  exact generated rc.4 suite; neither the new claim schema nor this metadata
+  asserts that a downstream manager release already implements schema 6.
+
+### Security
+
+- The build feature preserves the no-package-hooks boundary: manifests cannot
+  supply a shell, executable, argv, environment, output, build recipe, hook,
+  plugin, generator, test, post-build step, workspace, download, external
+  linker, or generic fallback. Managers never execute the compiled artifact
+  during install, upgrade, status, repair, rollback, or GC.
+- Compilation expands the trusted computing base to the manager, selected Go
+  toolchain, operating system isolation, canonicalization and hashing code,
+  and manager-owned staging/cache boundaries. Malicious source remains
+  untrusted compiler input and may target compiler defects or resource
+  exhaustion; implementations must enforce the specified host limits and
+  sandbox boundaries where supported.
+- Receipt and marker hashes detect consistency and drift but do not establish
+  provenance. Persistent cache reuse is allowed only after ownership,
+  permissions or DACL, containment, regular-file, and link-safety checks.
+  Same-principal, administrator/root, kernel, and hostile-storage compromise
+  remain outside the v1 protected-cache boundary.
+
